European Union ministers responsible for  energy policy have been unable to agree  on new rules to make the use of biofuels  me you've more sustainable and so help  cut greenhouse gas emissions but they  were able to narrow down some of the  sticking points also called indirect  land-use change unfortunately we have  had quite an exotic coalition of those  who are very ambitious in this respect  and those who not so ambitious and  therefore we did not reach a political  agreement but I think it is also  important to stress that we had a good  discussion and areas of concern were  identified and possible solutions also  where we are discussed in direct  land-use change occurs when farmland is  used to produce biofuels leading to food  or feed being grown in other areas like  forests which results in more carbon  dioxide in the atmosphere the aim of the  proposals was to minimize the  environmental impact of indirect land  use and encourage more advanced biofuels  onto the market The ministers also  endorsed a report on progress made  toward completing Europe's internal  energy market by next year's target days  it addresses aspects like the  development of energy infrastructure  ensuring investments energy efficiency  the price  a second report on the external  dimension of EU energy policy was also  rubber-stamped it covers developments in  the global energy landscape in recent  years progress made in EU policies in  this domain and looks and ways to  strengthen them
 

